[Music]  lumber has never been more expensive  two by fours are more than three times  their typical price  other wood products that we use to build  houses like plywood have also soared  the increases are thanks to an  unexpected boom in demand  even in the broad rally that has pushed  up commodity prices across the board  from crude oil to corn the dramatic  climb in wood prices stands out  lumber futures have more than tripled  since the rally began in april 2020.  no one expected wood prices to soar not  even sawmills lumber dealers  and others who profit from rising prices  here's how lumber prices climb to  records during the pandemic  who is profited and why those who grow  the trees haven't benefited at all  when the pandemic set in sawmill shut  down like many other businesses  before the lockdown it was shaping up to  be a strong home building season  but when the global economy ground to a  halt in 2020  new housing construction in the u.s  plummeted  at the onset of the pandemic lumber mill  shut down  dealers liquidated their inventories and  speculators started shorting  lumber futures expecting prices to  plunge no one thought that there was a  housing boom coming  or a remodeling boom hunker down  homeowners remodeled in mass  and low mortgage rates drove demand for  suburban housing  while restaurants raced to construct  outdoor dining accommodations  lumber and plywood started flying off  shelves the building boom lifted the  share price of home improvement giant's  lows and hope depot  ahead of the broader market wood was in  short supply  sawmills ramped up capacity but couldn't  catch up leading to a surge in prices  besides prices rising demand is such  that some people can't even get  the right amounts or the right products  of lumber and you're seeing this  throughout  you know across products from a 2x4 to  plywood to engineered products for home  builders  sawmills have been big beneficiaries  owners of southern mills have seen  profits skyrocket  and their share prices have surged  farther up the wood supply chain  those who grow the trees that become the  two by fours have been left out  timber growers supply mills with logs  which are sawed into lumber and mashed  into pulp  prices for logs and lumber usually move  in unison  but lately their prices have diverged  much of the nation's logs are harvested  in the u.s south  and southern growers have gained nothing  from the run-up in prices for finnish  lumber  earl and wanda bars have been growing  timber for 35 years in central georgia  they say prices for their timber have  not kept pace with the run-up in lumber  the landowner us we haven't benefited  from the  increase in the price of lumber when i  was a procurement forester  uh we were paying uh 25  a ton for a saw timber uh  on the stump 40 years ago and if you  bring that forward today  the market is 25 or less on the stump  today and that's in constant dollars  that's not taking into account inflation  and other  things that have gone up to grow those  trees the problem is that there's so  many trees  between 1980 and 2020 the volume of wood  per acre of timberland in many southern  counties has more than quadrupled  in the 1980s the federal government paid  a lot of landowners in the south to  plant trees  the government wanted to take cropland  out of rotation and  and to stop having a lot of corn and  soybeans so the prices would rise in  those markets  and they paid people to grow trees  because those trees would sit on the  land for decades  and just grow and accumulate value and  eventually be ready to be made into  things like lumber and plywood  decades later many of those trees are  being harvested which has flooded the  market with an abundance of timber  there's so many trees in fact that mills  can basically name their price for the  logs that they take in  and saw into boards the supply and  demand is so out of whack that  analysts and a lot of investors in  sawmill companies think it could be a  decade or two  before enough trees are cut down that  supply and demand  is back in balance even with the  increased demand we're seeing today for  wood products  earl barr says the consolidation of the  market has also made it difficult to  sell timber  20 years ago if we'd have a track of  timber that we would have put up for  bids we'd have gotten at least 10  independent bids competitive bids for  that track of wood  now we're seeing fewer than five if  you're lucky we're not looking at what  is happening to our markets  are we playing on a level playing field  are we  able to put our trees into a truly  competitive marketplace  analysts do expect lumber prices will  eventually go down  but with the housing market continuing  to run hot the price continues to rise

